    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. we checked the ink batch and the paper too and they seem totally fine. the weird thing is it's not a steady fade or a pattern. it's just these random little spots or small patches where the ink just isn't there, like it was snatched away. it's really frustrating because everything else is perfect. is there any other technical reason why the ink would just disappear during the transfer process?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Random, localized ink disappearance during sublimation, even with perfect equipment and supplies, is a tricky problem. It's less about a steady failure and more about tiny, unpredictable interactions. One possibility, though rare, is a microscopic issue with the heat press platen surface itself—perhaps a very small, localized imperfection or residue that is creating a micro-dead spot during the press cycle. Another possibility, and this is more of a wild guess, is a slight inconsistency in how the ink is being applied to the transfer paper, perhaps tiny air bubbles or uneven saturation in those specific areas. You might need to test a very small swatch area repeatedly to see if the failure point is reproducible under controlled, minute conditions.",
      "source": "target",
